# Example psm config file
#
# Copy it to:
#   ~/.config/psm/config   (priority 1, XDG standard)
#   ~/.psmrc               (priority 2, classic dotfile)
#
# Or point to a custom path:
#   export PSM_CONFIG=/path/to/my/config
#
# The file is sourced as bash, so standard bash syntax applies.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CyberArk infrastructure
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PSMP_HOST="psmp.example.com"
VAULT_USER="first.last"

# Per-host PSMP override: see the README, "Per-host PSMP_HOST override" section.
# Use `SetEnv PSM_PSMP_HOST=...` in ~/.ssh/config (supports Host LC*, etc.)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KeePassXC
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
KDBX_PATH="$HOME/Passwords.kdbx"
KDBX_GROUP="PSM"
VAULT_PASS_ENTRY="VaultPassword"

# GPG-encrypted master password (CLI mode, no GUI)
GPG_MASTER_FILE="$HOME/.psm-master.gpg"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LDAP / AD accounts — space-separated list
# The users listed here have a KeePassXC entry named after the user alone
# (not `user@host`). The lookup tries `user` first for these accounts, which
# avoids useless round trips when the same password works everywhere.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
LDAP_USER="ldap.user"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SSH / expect
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SSH_OPTS="-o StrictHostKeyChecking=no -o UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null -o ServerAliveInterval=15 -o ServerAliveCountMax=3 -o LogLevel=ERROR"
# EXPECT_TIMEOUT="30"

# Auto-sudo: 1 = automatically answers [sudo] prompts with the target
# password (handy on the target itself). 0 = manual sudo (recommended if you
# hop to other servers from the target — avoids sending the wrong password).
# AUTO_SUDO="1"
